========================================================= PROGRAM 9 =========================================================
Aim: 
To study and perform testing activities in an Agile project environment, demonstrating how 
testing is integrated into each iteration/sprint. 
Objective: 
1. To understand how testing is embedded in Agile sprints. 
2. To plan and execute test cases concurrently with development. 
3. To practice writing user stories and their acceptance criteria. 
4. To run manual/automated tests as part of Agile iteration. 
Problem Statement: 
In traditional software development, testing occurs at the end of the lifecycle, leading to 
late detection of defects and increased costs. Agile requires continuous testing within each 
sprint to ensure working, high-quality increments. This experiment focuses on 
implementing testing activities (planning, execution, automation) within an Agile iteration. 
Description: 
In Agile projects, testing is not a separate phase but an ongoing activity performed throughout 
the sprint. Testers, developers and product owners collaborate to define acceptance 
criteria for user stories. Testing activities include: 
 Writing test cases alongside user stories. 
 Performing unit testing, integration testing and acceptance testing within the 
sprint. 
 Using automation tools (like Selenium, JUnit, Pytest) to ensure quick feedback. 
 Continuous integration ensures tests run automatically on each commit. 
This experiment demonstrates how to take a small user story, write its acceptance criteria, 
create test cases, execute them (manually or automatically) and record results within a sprint. 
Procedure: 
1. Select a User Story: Choose a small functionality (e.g., “As a user, I can log in to the 
application”). 
2. Define Acceptance Criteria: Document clear conditions for the story to be 
considered “Done”. 
3. Write Test Cases: Prepare test scenarios and cases based on acceptance criteria. 
4. Develop the Feature: Developer implements the story during the sprint. 
5. Perform Unit Tests: Run unit tests immediately after coding. 
6. Perform Integration Tests: Verify the new functionality integrates with existing 
modules. 
7. Perform Acceptance Tests: Validate story against acceptance criteria with the 
Product Owner. 
8. Automate Tests (Optional): Use an automation tool (e.g., Selenium/Pytest) for 
repetitive checks. 
9. Log and Track Defects: Report issues in the Agile board (Jira/Trello). 
10. Review During Sprint Review: Demonstrate the working story with test results. 
Output: 
User Story: As a user, I can log in to the application. 
Acceptance Criteria: Verify that all acceptance criteria are met. 
User can log in with valid credentials. 
Error message shown for invalid login. 
Login redirects to dashboard. 
Test Summary: 
Unit Tests: Show that individual code components work correctly (15 passed / 0 failed) 
Integration Tests: Confirm the new feature works well with other modules (10 passed / 
0 failed). 
Acceptance Tests: Verify that all acceptance criteria are met. 
Defects: any bugs or issues discovered during testing are: 
 Logged in tools like Jira or Trello. 
 Assigned to developers for fixing. 
 Tracked until resolved. 
Status: Story completed and accepted in Sprint Review. 
Conclusion: 
Testing activities were successfully performed within the Agile project during the sprint. 
This demonstrated that Agile testing is continuous, collaborative, and aligned with user 
stories, ensuring high quality and quick feedback for each increment of software.

